<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-laplace-definition" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Definition of the Laplace Transform</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       DEFINITION: LAPLACE TRANSFORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <definition xml:id="def-laplace-transform">
    <title>The Laplace Transform</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose <m>f(t)</m> is a function defined for all <m>t \geq 0</m>.
        The <term>Laplace Transform</term> of <m>f</m> is the function <m>F(s)</m> defined by
        <me>F(s) = \mathcal{L}\{f(t)\} = \int_0^{\infty} e^{-st} f(t) \, dt.</me>
        The transform is defined for all values of <m>s</m> for which the integral converges.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <p>
    A few things are worth noticing right away. The input to the Laplace transform is a function
    of <m>t</m>, and the output is a function of a new variable <m>s</m>. So the Laplace transform
    takes us out of the world of <m>t</m> and into the world of <m>s</m>. We typically use capital
    letters for the transformed function (like <m>Y(s)</m>) and lowercase for the original
    (like <m>y(t)</m>).
  </p>

  <p>
    In the next few pages, we will get practice finding Laplace and inverse Laplace transforms
    of functions. We will see that taking the Laplace transform of a differential equation
    turns it into an <em>algebraic</em> equation in <m>s</m>, which is often much easier to solve!
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       TRANSITION: TABLE COMING LATER
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    In practice, we'll use a <em>Laplace transform table</em> (coming soon!) to look up transforms
    rather than computing the integral every time. You won't be asked to derive transforms from
    scratch going forward. But it's worth seeing where some of the table entries come from!
  </p>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXPLORATION: TWO BASIC LAPLACE TRANSFORMS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <exploration xml:id="exploration-laplace-basic">
    <title>Computing Two Laplace Transforms from the Definition</title>

    <task xml:id="task-laplace-of-1">
      <title>The Laplace Transform of <m>f(t) = 1</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
         Let's use the definition to show that
          <me>\mathcal{L}\{1\} = \frac{1}{s}, \quad s \gt 0.</me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>  <title>Work (can skim)</title>
        <p>
          Plugging <m>f(t) = 1</m> into the definition and converting to a limit:
          <md>
            <mrow>F(s) \amp= \int_0^{\infty} e^{-st} \cdot 1 \, dt
                        = \lim_{b \to \infty} \left[ -\frac{1}{s} e^{-st} \right]_0^b</mrow>
            <mrow>      \amp= \lim_{b \to \infty} \frac{-1}{s}\left(e^{-sb} - e^0\right)</mrow>
            <mrow>      \amp= \frac{-1}{s}(0 - 1) = \frac{1}{s}.</mrow>
          </md>
          The limit <m>e^{-sb} \to 0</m> as <m>b \to \infty</m> requires <m>s \gt 0</m>,
          so the transform is defined for <m>s \gt 0</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-laplace-of-eat">
      <title>The Laplace Transform of <m>f(t) = e^{at}</m></title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          We now use the definition to show that
          <me>\mathcal{L}\{e^{at}\} = \frac{1}{s-a}, \quad s \gt a.</me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>   <title>Work (can skim)</title>
        <p>
          Plugging <m>f(t) = e^{at}</m> into the definition and combining the exponentials:
          <md>
            <mrow>F(s) \amp= \int_0^{\infty} e^{-st} e^{at} \, dt
                        = \lim_{b \to \infty} \int_0^b e^{-(s-a)t} \, dt</mrow>
            <mrow>      \amp= \lim_{b \to \infty} \left[ \frac{-1}{s-a} e^{-(s-a)t} \right]_0^b</mrow>
            <mrow>      \amp= \lim_{b \to \infty} \frac{-1}{s-a}\left(e^{-(s-a)b} - e^0\right)</mrow>
            <mrow>      \amp= \frac{-1}{s-a}(0 - 1) = \frac{1}{s-a}.</mrow>
          </md>
          The limit <m>e^{-(s-a)b} \to 0</m> as <m>b \to \infty</m> requires <m>s - a \gt 0</m>,
          i.e., <m>s \gt a</m>.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </exploration>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Definition:</alert>
          the Laplace transform is the improper integral
          <m>\mathcal{L}\{f(t)\} = \displaystyle\int_0^{\infty} e^{-st} f(t) \, dt</m>.
          It takes a function of <m>t</m> and produces a function of <m>s</m>.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>You won't compute from scratch going forward:</alert>
          we'll use a table of known transforms. The two examples above are the source
          of the first two entries in that table.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Two results from the exploration:</alert>
          <m>\mathcal{L}\{1\} = \dfrac{1}{s}</m> (for <m>s \gt 0</m>),
          and <m>\mathcal{L}\{e^{at}\} = \dfrac{1}{s-a}</m> (for <m>s \gt a</m>).
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

